<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paul I Šubić of Bribir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1245–<a href="/years/1312">1312</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paul I Šubić of Bribir was a Croatian nobleman who became ban of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> in the 1270s and the most powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> of the kingdom. Ruling much of Croatia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> almost independently, he styled himself lord of Bosnia and backed the Angevin Charles Robert for the Hungarian crown. His family's power declined soon after his death in 1312.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Paul was the eldest son of Stephen II of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subic-family">Šubić family</a>, the most influential noble house of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>. His father had helped King Béla IV in the war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> in 1242 and received the County of Bribir as a hereditary grant in 1251, and his mother was probably related to the Árpád dynasty. Paul inherited the title of count of Bribir and first appears in 1272 as Podestà of Trogir. In 1273 he became ban of the maritime regions, was removed in 1274 over his part in the quarrels between Trogir and Split, and returned in 1275.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>With his brothers Mladen I and George I, Paul brought almost all the coastal cities south of the Velebit Mountain under Šubić rule by 1278, from seats at Bribir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skradin">Skradin</a>, Klis and Ostrovica. His campaign against the pirate Kačić family of Omiš drew him into war with the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, ended by treaties in 1290 and 1294, and into friendship with the House of Anjou of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. In the succession crisis after 1290 he was the main ally of the Angevins; in 1300 he met Charles Robert at Split and took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a>, where loyal nobles recognised him as king.</p><p>Paul took the title of lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> in 1299, captured the region of Hum from the Serbian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-milutin">Stefan Milutin</a> in 1301, and after Mladen I was killed in 1304 led an army into Bosnia, restoring his rule by February 1305. Styling himself ban of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>, he ruled as a de facto independent prince, kept chancelleries at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skradin">Skradin</a> and Bribir, and issued coins of Bosnian silver modelled on the Venetian grosso.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In March 1311 an uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zadar</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> was aided by an army under his son Mladen II, who was proclaimed Count of Zadar and Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>. Peace talks with Venice began in April 1312, and Paul died on 1 May while they continued. He was buried in the Franciscan church of St. Mary in Bribir, the family's funerary church, and was succeeded by Mladen II. Under his rule new dioceses were founded at Šibenik, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomislavgrad">Duvno</a> and Omiš, and the Šubićs invoked the memory of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-zvonimir">Demetrius Zvonimir</a> to present their rule as continuing that of the old Croatian kings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paul I Šubić of Bribir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Powerful nobles at times ruled almost alone: <b>Paul I Šubić of Bribir</b>, ban from 1274, became hereditary ban in the succession war of the 1290s, took Bosnia in 1299, issued his own money and helped bring Charles I of Anjou to the throne.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-croatia-1102-1526">Kingdom of Croatia (1102-1526) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Croatia in personal union with Hungary. Died in Bribir, Šibenik-Knin County, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.93,15.84&amp;year=1288" aria-label="Bribir, Šibenik-Knin County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4930 608 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Paul I Šubić of Bribir"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5380 878h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5380 878h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=43.93,15.84&amp;year=1288">What was here in 1288? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
